From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 3 Sep 2026 11:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] freedreno/a5xx: use bypass for multisampled render targets

a5xx has no GMEM resolve.  emit_gmem2mem_surf() stores GMEM back to
system memory with the resolve hardcoded off, and the correct condition
commented out beside it:

	//	bool msaa_resolve = pfb->samples > 1;
	bool msaa_resolve = false;

So a multisampled GMEM buffer is written to its single-sample
destination without the samples ever being averaged.

Captured from the hardware command stream on an a540 (chip_id 0x5040001,
FD_RD_DUMP + cffdump), one such render pass reads:

	RB_CNTL:            { WIDTH = 128 | HEIGHT = 32 }
	RB_RAS_MSAA_CNTL:   { SAMPLES = MSAA_FOUR }
	RB_DEST_MSAA_CNTL:  { SAMPLES = MSAA_FOUR }
	... draws ...
	RB_BLIT_CNTL:       { BUF = BLIT_MRT0 }
	RB_BLIT_DST_PITCH:  512			/* 128 px * 4 bytes, 1 sample */
	RB_CLEAR_CNTL:      { MASK = 0 }	/* no MSAA_RESOLVE */

The destination pitch is single-sample while GMEM holds four samples per
pixel, and MSAA_RESOLVE is clear, so the store reads interleaved samples
as pixels.  Coverage belonging to one row is spread along the row: a
filled shape gains a horizontal band at its widest extent.  Shapes with
axis-aligned edges have almost no partially covered pixels and survive
it; anything with a long diagonal edge does not.

This is not an exotic path.  Skia renders complex paths into small
multisampled mask surfaces, so it hits ordinary web content -- a 48px
triangular warning glyph in a composited layer renders with a 120x24
pixel bar across it, while the axis-aligned glyphs beside it are
pixel-exact.  Rendering the same page with FD_MESA_DEBUG=sysmem is
pixel-correct.

Simply setting RB_CLEAR_CNTL.MSAA_RESOLVE is not a fix and makes the
image worse, with or without splitting the RAS and DEST sample counts
the way a4xx does for its resolve pass; the rest of the sequence the
resolve needs is not known.  Until it is, keep the tiled path away from
multisampled framebuffers and let bypass handle them, the same way
layered rendering and tessellation are already handled.  The affected
surfaces are small, so the bandwidth cost is slight, and correctness
beats a resolve that is not implemented.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c
index 9f7124d..1ef750c 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c
@@ -754,6 +754,17 @@ fd_gmem_render_tiles(struct fd_batch *batch)
       sysmem = true;
    }
 
+   /* a5xx cannot resolve a multisampled GMEM buffer on store: the resolve in
+    * emit_gmem2mem_surf() is hardcoded off because the sequence it needs is
+    * not known, so the samples reach the single-sample destination unaveraged
+    * and partially covered pixels smear along their row.  Bypass resolves
+    * correctly, so use it for multisampled framebuffers.
+    */
+   if (is_a5xx(ctx->screen) && (pfb->samples > 1)) {
+      assert(ctx->emit_sysmem_prep);
+      sysmem = true;
+   }
+
    fd_reset_wfi(batch);
 
    ctx->stats.batch_total++;
